  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe core idea is to use a gradient-free, derivative-free optimization method called Stochastic Perturbation for Stochastic Approximation (SPSA) to navigate the complex optimization landscape of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Unlike gradient-based local optimization methods that often get trapped in local minima, SPSA explores the solution space more robustly and systematically. The approach uses random perturbations to estimate gradients, enabling global exploration while still focusing on promising regions. This method is particularly suitable for non-convex, high-dimensional problems like ours, where the objective function has many local minima with similar values. The key innovations are:\n\n- A systematic perturbation and projection strategy that maintains feasibility constraints for the integral and domain\n- Extensive reuse of gradient information through vectorized random perturbations\n- Efficient fallback mechanisms when performance deviates from expectations\n- Dimensionality-robust optimization using stochastic information\n\nThis approach aims to significantly improve upon the current record of 0.3808 by explicitly targeting lower correlation values through controlled exploration. The main computational cost comes from the full correlation calculations, which grow quadratically with the number of points. This trade-off between solution quality and computation is carefully managed.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nRepresent h as a real-valued vector of length n with components in [0,1], summing to exactly n/2. The optimization objective is:\n\nC5(h) = max_k (1/n) * |\u222b_0\u00b2 h(t) (1 - h(t+k)) dt|\n\nNotably, the integral is defined over the [0,2] domain and for each shift k. The evaluation must account for boundary effects when k causes the shifted function to fall outside [0,2].\n\nThe optimization enforces these constraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u03a3h[i] = n/2\n\nSPSA is applied with:\n- Two-mode operation: Explorative and exploitative phases\n- Variable-length binary representation in function evaluations\n- Strict gradient-free operation using central differences\n- Feasibility projection mechanism to maintain integral constraint\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialize (dimension initializer)\n- Set n = 400 (fixed resolution, as the input parent solution is at this resolution)\n- Project initial_h_values to satisfy \u03a3h = 200 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n- Set function evaluation budget based on timing analysis (two evaluations per main iteration)\n\nb. Core search procedure (clique optimization with stochastic focus)\n    Stage 1: Stochastic perturbation\n        For each iteration:\n            Generate random direction vector w \u2208 {-1,1}\u207f\n            Calculate C5 at nearby points perturbed by w\n            Estimate directional derivative\n            Update h along negative gradient approximation\n            Project using integral-preserving projection\n\n    Stage 2: Adaptive step size control\n        Maintain two step size regimes:\n            Exploration: Large steps with high noise tolerance\n            Exploitation: Small steps with gradient refinement\n\n    Stage 3: Budget-aware termination\n        Monitor wall clock time:\n            If elapsed time exceeds 95% of budget: \n                Reduce to most recent solution\n            If improvement stalls: \n                Switch to grid checkpoint search\n\n    Stage 4: Performance calibration\n        Maintain track of:\n            - Best score floor/ceiling\n            - Correlation profile at peaks\n            - Gradient variance across directions\n\nc. Feasibility projection (exact integral enforcement)\n    Input: Problem vector h (n-vector), target sum S = n/2\n    Output: Feasible vector\n    Procedure:\n        diff = current_sum - S\n        while abs(diff) > 1e-6 and max attempts:\n            Adjust values proportional to diff\n            Clip to [0,1] bounds\n            Perform limited inequality repair\n            Normalize to target sum\n\nd. Downhill checking \n    After formulating elite solutions, perform downhill move if:\n        Improved_C5_variance > threshold and no early wall clock limit\n        Minimal runtime analysis detects opportunity\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\na. Core parameters:\n- Perturbation magnitude a = 0.618 (fixed)\n- Direction vector distribution: Uniform {-1,1}\n- Update hyperparameter \u03b3 = 0.8 for exponential decay\n- Projection steps: Up to 30 attempts\n- Initial evaluations: 12 per direction\n\nb. Update configuration:\n- Base step count: 1000\n- Evaluation frequency: After every 15 updates\n- Checkpoint frequency: After every 100 updates\n- Cooling schedule: Independent of temperature\n\nc. Dynamic adjustments:\n- If gradient variance drops below 0.1: Adapt to direction-enriched sampling\n- If gradient variance exceeds 0.5: Use broadened neighborhood search\n- Early termination if consecutive 10 evaluations show no progress\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness\n\nDominant cost:\n- Per-evaluation: O(n\u00b2) computation (two evaluations per iteration)\n- Estimated time: 1.6 seconds per 400-point evaluation\n- Maximum evaluations: 2000 (1600 function calls) for 1300-second budget\n\nFeasibility on deadline issues:\n- If any evaluation exceeds 2 seconds: \n    Downgrade to binary neighbor subset\n- Budget alert at 5% remaining: Trigger robust projection fallback\n\nComplexity variance handling:\n- Binary tree replacement scheduling\n- Parallelized expression vector subset\n\nRecovery protocol:\n- After 400 evaluations with no clear improvement:\n    Reduce resolution temporarily (to 200 points)\n    Re-identify elite patterns (through multidirectional research)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nPost-optimization checks:\n- Verify \u03a3h[i] = 200 with 1e-6 tolerance\n- Check 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Re-evaluate final solution with precision (not automatic fallback)\n\nOutput guarantees:\n- At least the parent solution quality\n- No runtime failures allowed (determined fallback)\n\nThe algorithm specifically targets lower bounds through:\n- Correlation profile reduction portals\n- Cross-validated flowering searches\n- Sensitive threshold decision points\n- Minimal restart procedures\n\nEach returned vector satisfies:\n- Exact integral (n/2)\n- Real-valued components (no integerization)\n- Unique valid configuration\n\nObjective evaluation compares with:\n- Baseline Erd\u0151s (assessed via task evaluator)\n\nThe optimization carefully handles the boundary effects by:\n- Not relying on circular assumptions (looking exclusively at direction magnitude)\n- Willingness to shift to adjacent resolutions if stuck\n- Explicit preservation of Erd\u0151s foundation\n\nThe code implementation aligns with:\n- Pre-imported helper utilities (used directly without re-implementation)\n- Dimension flexibility (but fixed to 400 for integration)\n- Stochastic optimization without gradient calculation\n- Clear termination conditions\n\nThe core optimization balances exploration and exploitation through a custom-designed stochastic framework tailored to the non-convex, high-dimensional nature of the Erd\u0151s problem.",
